From 1274b924f3503afe77171fe5ffd620279cd6ac43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 17:07:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4: spin up to 20 us before sleeping on Eden's host locks;
 wake the timing thread only for a new earliest event

The kernel's scheduler and hardware timer locks (KSpinLock), the emulated cores' context locks
(PhysicalCore::m_guard) and CoreTiming's event queue lock are std::mutexes, which on the PS4 sleep
in the kernel as soon as they are found taken; waking costs far more than they are held for.
Common::SpinThenLock (common/ps4_spin_lock.h) tries again for up to 20 us first, bounded so a
waiter never keeps a holder off its core for long. Each lock class counts its contention
(PS4Perf::LockStats). ScheduleEvent wakes the timing thread only when the new event is the
earliest in the queue. Other platforms lock as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01REmpC53sSK3VTcKMxWFSSb
